1. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 7, 4, or 18. Teacher checks method + units.
2. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 5, 12, or 35. Teacher checks method + units.
3. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 7, 10, or 16. Teacher checks method + units.
4. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 14, 10, or 33. Teacher checks method + units.
5. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 7, 9, or 14. Teacher checks method + units.
6. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 14, 9, or 31. Teacher checks method + units.

</details>

## Exit ticket

1. In one sentence, what does “Double Number Lines” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?
2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to dropping a negative sign when comparing…).
3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.

*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*

