1. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 5, -3, or 30. Teacher checks method + units.
2. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 12, 5, or 47. Teacher checks method + units.
3. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 5, 4, or 28. Teacher checks method + units.
4. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 11, 1, or 13. Teacher checks method + units.
5. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 13, 8, or 34. Teacher checks method + units.
6. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 11, 8, or 11. Teacher checks method + units.

</details>

## Exit ticket

1. In one sentence, what does “Four Quadrants” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?
2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to dropping a negative sign when comparing…).
3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.

*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*

## Stretch (optional)

